---
/**
 * Section 02 — scale, answered as a claim rather than a caveat.
 *
 * Three things are deliberately absent.
 *
 * No weather runtime: three different values were committed for that one
 * command, so no figure for it is published until a single isolated run
 * settles it.
 *
 * No second and third wall clock beside the first: the shipped runs normalize
 * to very different coupled-steps-per-second on the same 1024-cell layer, and
 * printing them together under a heading called "Scale" invites arithmetic
 * whose answer is a leg count rather than a throughput.
 *
 * No core-saturation claim: the only fan-out cost gate is five branches on
 * sixteen cores, which is under-subscribed and cannot demonstrate it. The
 * sentence is scoped to what was measured.
 */
import { MACHINE } from '../../consts';
---
<section class="cfd-sec page" data-rise>
  <header class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-coord">
      <span class="ix">02 / 05</span><span class="lb">Scale</span>
    </p>
    <h2>No cluster required</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Load a solved case, fork it, fly the roster on the machine already on your
      desk.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="panel corner-brackets body-panel rise">
    <p class="body">
      That works because the branches never interact. A roster has no shared
      mutable state, no cross-thread floating-point reduction and no random
      draws, so it is embarrassingly parallel. Dispatch is a scoped fork-join,
      one chunk per available core; no thread pool, no Rayon, no external
      scheduler. Concurrency also does not change the answer, and a regression
      test holds that line: a two-branch fan-out must equal a sequential fork
      chain of the same pause, bit for bit.
    </p>

    <ul class="nums">
      <li>Whole retropulsion descent, 2516 coupled steps across four legs with a five-branch mid-burn fan-out: 337.5 s, 18.3 MB peak resident set.</li>
      <li>Corridor, one march to the onset plus seventeen forked worlds: 44.4 s against a 600 s budget.</li>
      <li>Five concurrent branches at 1.09× the per-step cost of the unforked run they came from, so the whole roster cost about one branch rather than five.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="src">All at <code>FloatType = f64</code>, release build, on {MACHINE}.</p>

    <p class="body">
      Cores are not the binding constraint; memory is. Forking itself is O(1),
      since every branch enters by reference with no tensor copied. What a
      branch pays for is its first write: the per-cell scalar vectors, the
      navigation engine and the provenance log. So per-branch cost is O(cells),
      and 18.3 MB at 1024 cells is the number to size a larger fan-out against.
    </p>

    <p class="body">
      That 1024 is the counterfactual examples, not the ceiling. The
      incompressible solver runs cylinder verification at 256², and has been
      marched at 512², 262 thousand cells, at 1.22 s a step. Nothing here has
      been run at production mesh scale, and the fork machinery is independent
      of the grid it forks.
    </p>

    <p class="body">
      Distributed execution is a stated non-goal. GPU work over the tensor-train
      layer is deferred rather than promised. The <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a>
      carries both with the reasoning.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .cfd-sec { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .sec-head { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .sec-head h2 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-3); }
  .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  .body-panel { padding: var(--space-5); }
  .body { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }

  .nums { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); }
  .nums li {
    color: var(--fg-1);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    font-size: var(--t-mono-sm);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .src {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
  }
</style>
